%[text] %[text:anchor:T_CFF3DFB5] # Air Track Collisions Lab: Theory
%[text] [⇦ Return to the main menu](<matlab: open(fullfile(currentProject().RootFolder,'MainMenu.m'))>)
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_33632D6A] ## Momentum
%[text] The **momentum** of a body is defined as the product of its mass and velocity:
%[text]  $\\vec{p} = m \\vec{v}, \\qquad (1)${"altText":"\vec{p} = m \vec{v}, \qquad (1)"}
%[text] where $m${"altText":"m"} is the body's mass and $\\vec{v} = \[v\_x, v\_y, v\_z\]${"altText":"\vec{v} = \[v\_x, v\_y, v\_z\]"} its velocity vector. The momentum vector, $\\vec{p}${"altText":"\vec{p}"}, points in the direction of the velocity vector. The magnitude of the vector is proportional to the mass, as illustrated below.
%[text]  ![Mass mmm moving right with velocity vvv; momentum ppp shown in the same direction.](text:image:4bd2)
%[text] A body's momentum can be thought of as its impulse: how much of an impact it will have when colliding with another body. Oddly, momentum is not a direct measure of the motion energy of the body.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_EA7E46A1] ## Kinetic and Potential Energy
%[text] The **kinetic energy**, $E\_k${"altText":"E\_k"}, is the energy a body has due to motion. It is defined as
%[text]  $E\_k = \\frac{1}{2} m v^2 \\qquad (2)${"altText":"E\_k = \frac{1}{2} m v^2 \qquad (2)"}
%[text] where $v${"altText":"v"} is the magnitude of the velocity vector: $v = \\sqrt{ v\_x^2 + v\_y^2 + v\_z^2 }${"altText":"v = \sqrt{ v\_x^2 + v\_y^2 + v\_z^2 }"}. This magnitude $v${"altText":"v"} is commonly referred to as the body's **speed**. Note that the kinetic energy has units of energy. For example, a 1 kg mass moving at 1 m/s has 1 Joule of kinetic energy: $E\_k = 1 \\; \\mathrm{kg}\\cdot 1 \\; \\mathrm{m^2/s^2} = 1 \\; \\mathrm{J}${"altText":"E\_k = 1 ; \mathrm{kg}\cdot 1 ; \mathrm{m^2\/s^2} = 1 ; \mathrm{J}"}.
%[text] The **gravitational potential energy** of a body is the stored energy a body has due to its position in a gravitational field. The potential energy of a mass near the Earth's surface is
%[text] $U = m g h${"altText":"U = m g h"}
%[text] where $g = 9.81 \\; \\mathrm{m/s^2}${"altText":"g = 9.81 ; \mathrm{m\/s^2}"} is the acceleration due to gravity near the surface of the earth and $h${"altText":"h"} is the height above a reference surface. 
%[text]  ![Mass mmm at height hhh above a reference level, representing gravitational potential energy U=mghU = mghU=mgh.](text:image:1682)
%[text] If the mass is dropped a distance $\\Delta h${"altText":"\Delta h"}, it will gain kinetic energy equal to the amount of potential energy lost (assuming an ideal system without external forces).
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_82ADC4BA] ## Collisions
%[text] In an isolated system (i.e., in the absence of external forces), the total momentum of the system does not change. This is known as the **conservation of momentum**. For example, consider an isolated system of two masses: $m\_1${"altText":"m\_1"} and $m\_2${"altText":"m\_2"}. If the masses then collide, their total momentum after impact will be equal to their total momentum before impact:
%[text]  $m\_1 \\vec{v}\_{1,i} +  m\_2 \\vec{v}\_{2,i} =  m\_1 \\vec{v}\_{1,f} +  m\_2 \\vec{v}\_{2,f} \\qquad (3)${"altText":"m\_1 \vec{v}\_{1,i} +  m\_2 \vec{v}\_{2,i} =  m\_1 \vec{v}\_{1,f} +  m\_2 \vec{v}\_{2,f} \qquad (3)"}
%[text] where $\\vec{v}\_{n,i}${"altText":"\vec{v}\_{n,i}"} denotes the velocity of the $n^{th${"altText":"n^{th"} mass before collision and $\\vec{v}\_{n,f}${"altText":"\vec{v}\_{n,f}"} after collision.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E745ADE2] #### Elastic collisions
%[text] When the two masses collide, momentum will always be conserved. However, the kinetic energy will not always be conserved. Some of it may be transferred into another form of energy. In some collisions, known as **elastic collisions**, kinetic energy **is** conserved (along with momentum). For a two-mass system:
%[text]  $\\frac{1}{2}m\_1 v\_{1,i}^2 +  \\frac{1}{2}m\_2 v\_{2,i}^2 =  \\frac{1}{2}m\_1 v\_{1,f}^2 +  \\frac{1}{2}m\_2 v\_{2,f}^2 \\qquad (4)${"altText":"\frac{1}{2}m\_1 v\_{1,i}^2 +  \frac{1}{2}m\_2 v\_{2,i}^2 =  \frac{1}{2}m\_1 v\_{1,f}^2 +  \frac{1}{2}m\_2 v\_{2,f}^2 \qquad (4)"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![An elastic collision](text:image:55c6)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *An elastic collision*
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_62C5C53F] #### Inelastic collisions
%[text] In an inelastic collision, kinetic energy is not conserved (although the momentum is still conserved). For example, consider a collision where the two bodies become fused during the collision.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![ An inelastic collision](text:image:2fc7)
%[text]{"align":"center"}  *An inelastic collision*
%[text] In this case, the velocity of the two bodies is the same after the collision: $\\vec{v}\_{1,f} = \\vec{v}\_{2,f} = \\vec{v}\_f${"altText":"\vec{v}\_{1,f} = \vec{v}\_{2,f} = \vec{v}\_f"}. The momentum conservation equation (3) becomes:
%[text] $m\_1 \\vec{v}\_{1,i} +  m\_2 \\vec{v}\_{2,i} =  (m\_1 + m\_2) \\vec{v}\_{f}${"altText":"m\_1 \vec{v}\_{1,i} +  m\_2 \vec{v}\_{2,i} =  (m\_1 + m\_2) \vec{v}\_{f}"}.
%[text] This equation has only one solution for $\\vec{v}\_f${"altText":"\vec{v}\_f"}: 
%[text] $\\vec{v}\_{f} = \\frac{m\_1 \\vec{v}\_{1,i} +  m\_2 \\vec{v}\_{2,i} }{m\_1 + m\_2}${"altText":"\vec{v}\_{f} = \frac{m\_1 \vec{v}\_{1,i} +  m\_2 \vec{v}\_{2,i} }{m\_1 + m\_2}"}.
%[text] This solution is incompatible with the conservation of energy in almost all cases.
